<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Dance · 1792 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Carmagnole</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song and wild dance of the French Revolution, named after the short jacket worn by working people in Paris.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the sans-culottes of revolutionary Paris and later French revolutionaries</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Paris, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first sung in August 1792; added to in 1830, 1848, 1863–64 and 1882–83</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous (first sung in August 1792, with verses added in the 19th century))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a>&quot; is a French song created and made popular during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, together with a wild dance of the same name that may have come into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with the Piedmontese. It was first sung in August 1792, and new verses were added in the revolutionary years of 1830, 1848, 1863–64 and 1882–83. Its authors are not known. The name comes from the short jacket worn by the working-class militants called sans-culottes, taken from a Piedmontese peasant costume named after the town of Carmagnola.</p><p>It was sung mainly as a rallying cry and an entertainment of the revolutionaries, and it mocked King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and the monarchists. After the storming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries Palace</a> on the night of 9–10 August 1792, when the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> forced the king to flee to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> and massacred his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a>, the crowd danced and sang the Carmagnole to celebrate. It was also used against the Revolution's opponents: a common punishment was to make nobles, princes and clergy sing and dance it. It served as a battle song as well; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes">Battle of Jemappes</a> on 6 November 1792 the sans-culottes in the army charged singing &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a>&quot;, and the victory brought all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> under the revolutionary armies.</p><p>Singing was a central way of taking part in the Revolution, and the French took pride in it as a source of their success. The Carmagnole was sung after victories and at popular festivals such as the planting of liberty trees, of which some 60,000 were planted during the Revolution. Like &quot;Ah! ça ira&quot;, it was popular because its words were simple enough for people who could not read to learn and join in.</p><p>The song outlived the Revolution. The exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-schurz">Carl Schurz</a> recalled French friends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> dancing the Carmagnole after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s coup of 2 December 1851. On the Greek island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a>, then under Ottoman rule, it gave its name to the Karmanioloi, a faction inspired by the Revolution's liberal and democratic ideals that opposed the old landholding notables and led Samos to join the Greek War of Independence in 1821. It appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>'s A Tale of Two Cities, Baroness Orczy's The Scarlet Pimpernel and Gertrud von Le Fort's The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> at the Scaffold, is sung in Umberto Giordano's opera Andrea Chénier and Eugene O'Neill's The Iceman Cometh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> set it against &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot; in Les Misérables.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A mocking song of triumph over the king, the queen and their supporters, sung with a wild dance. Its mood is sarcastic and defiant, and its words were simple enough for anyone to learn and join in.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Madam’ Véto avait promis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Madame Veto had promised</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De faire égorger tout Paris</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To have all Paris's throats cut</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais le coup a manqué,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But the blow failed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Grâce à nos canonniers.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thanks to our gunners.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dansons la carmagnole</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's dance the carmagnole</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vive le son, vive le son</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the sound, long live the sound</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dansons la carmagnole,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's dance the carmagnole,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vive le son du canon !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the sound of the cannon!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Monsieur Véto avait promis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Monsieur Veto had promised</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">D’être fidèle à sa patrie ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To be faithful to his country;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais il y a manqué.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But he failed in it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ne faisons plus quartier.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us give no more quarter.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Antoinette avait résolu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Antoinette had resolved</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De nous fair' tomber sur le cul ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To make us fall on our arse;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais son coup a manqué</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But her blow failed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle a le nez cassé.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She has broken her nose.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 56 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Son mari, se croyant vainqueur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her husband, believing himself the victor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Connaissait peu notre valeur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Knew little of our courage.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Va, Louis, gros paour,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go, Louis, big lout [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Du Temple, dans la tour.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the Temple, into the tower.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les Suisses avaient promis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Swiss had promised</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qu’ils feraient feu sur nos amis ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That they would fire on our friends;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais, comme ils ont sauté,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But how they were blown up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Comme ils ont tous dansé !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How they all danced!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand Antoinette vit la tour,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When Antoinette saw the tower,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ell' voulut fair' demi-tour ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She wanted to turn back;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle avait mal au cœur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She was sick at heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De se voir sans honneur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To see herself without honour.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Lorsque Louis vit fossoyer,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When Louis saw digging,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À ceux qu’il voyait travailler</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To those he saw working</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il disait que pour peu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He said that it would take little</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il était dans ce lieu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For him to be in that place.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le patriote a pour amis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The patriot has as friends</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Toutes les bonnes gens du pays ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the good people of the country;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais ils se soutiendront</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But they will hold each other up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tous au son du canon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All at the sound of the cannon.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’aristocrate a pour amis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The aristocrat has as friends</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tous les royalist's à Paris</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the royalists in Paris</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ils vous les soutiendront</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They will stand by them for you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout comm' de vrais poltrons.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just like true cowards.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La gendarm'rie avait promis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The gendarmerie had promised</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qu’elle soutiendrait la patrie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That it would support the country</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais ils n’ont pas manqué</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But they did not fail</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Au son du canonnier.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>At the sound of the cannoneer.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Amis, restons toujours unis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Friends, let us always stay united</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ne craignons pas nos ennemis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us not fear our enemies</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S’ils vienn'nt nous attaquer,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If they come to attack us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous les ferons sauter.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will blow them up.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Oui, je suis sans-culotte, moi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, I am a sans-culotte, I am</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">En dépit des amis du roi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In spite of the king's friends</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vivent les Marseillois,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the Marseillais,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les Bretons et nos lois !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Bretons and our laws!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Oui, nous nous souviendrons toujours,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, we will always remember</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des sans-culottes des faubourgs</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sans-culottes of the faubourgs</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À leur santé buvons ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us drink to their health;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vivent ces bons lurons.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live these good fellows.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous (1792); the text in Pierre Larousse's Grand dictionnaire universel (1868). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/La_Carmagnole_(dictionnaire_universel_1868)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1, 3.1: 'Madame Véto' and 'Monsieur Véto' are Marie Antoinette and Louis XVI, named for the king's veto.</li><li>line 2.2: 'le son' is the sound (of the cannon, as line 2.4 says).</li><li>line 5.3: 'paour' is old popular French for a lout or peasant; the sense is not certain.</li><li>line 5.4: the Temple, the fortress in Paris where the royal family was imprisoned in August 1792.</li><li>line 6.3-6.4: 'sauter' and 'danser' are slang for being blown up or killed: the Swiss Guards killed at the Tuileries, 10 August 1792.</li><li>line 9.3: 'se soutiendront' can also mean 'will stand firm'.</li><li>line 10.3: 'vous les soutiendront' is ironic: 'they'll back them up for you'.</li><li>line 11.3-11.4: the sense is unclear; perhaps the gendarmes did not fail to turn up when the cannon sounded.</li><li>line 14.2: the faubourgs, the working districts on the edge of Paris (Saint-Antoine, Saint-Marcel).</li><li>line 8.4: 'pour peu' here: it would take little (for him to lie in the pit being dug).</li></ul></details></section>
