<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Ravachole (La Nouvelle Carmagnole)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">anarchist version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Carmagnole des royalistes</span><span class="sg-chain-h">royalist version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Tale of Two Cities (Charles Dickens)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">mentioned in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Scarlet Pimpernel (Baroness Orczy)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">mentioned in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Song at the Scaffold (Gertrud von Le Fort)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">plays an important role in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Andrea Chénier (Umberto Giordano)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by the chorus in Act III</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Iceman Cometh (Eugene O'Neill)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in the closing dialogue</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Les Misérables (Victor Hugo)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">contrasted with La Marseillaise</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The 42nd Parallel (John Dos Passos)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by revolutionary workers in Mexico</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Foucault's Pendulum (Umberto Eco)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">mentioned in</span></li></ul></section>
